  mortgage_kb_explain_pti_gel_low_income: 'For a loan in lari, if your monthly income after tax is below 1,500 GEL, the National Bank limits total monthly debt payments to 25% of that income.',
  mortgage_kb_explain_pti_gel_high_income: 'For a loan in lari, if your monthly income after tax is 1,500 GEL or more, the National Bank limits total monthly debt payments to 50% of that income.',
  mortgage_kb_explain_pti_fx_low_income: 'For a loan in a foreign currency, if your monthly income after tax is below the equivalent of 1,500 GEL, the limit is stricter at 20% of that income.',
  mortgage_kb_explain_pti_fx_high_income: 'For a loan in a foreign currency, if your monthly income after tax is the equivalent of 1,500 GEL or more, the limit is 30% of that income. It is stricter than for a loan in lari because your income and your debt would be in different currencies.',
  mortgage_kb_explain_ltv_gel: 'For a loan in lari, the National Bank allows a lender to finance up to 90% of the property value, which means a down payment of at least 10%.',
  mortgage_kb_explain_ltv_fx: 'For a loan in a foreign currency, the limit is 70% of the property value, which means a down payment of at least 30%.',
  mortgage_kb_explain_effective_rate_methodology: 'The National Bank publishes its own calculator for the real yearly cost, and Homatch asks for the same inputs. The figure here uses the standard cash-flow method consumer-credit regulators use. It is labelled as calculated, and a figure your bank states itself always takes precedence.',
